-- 718 more medicines, from the public drug registries.
--
-- Names and marketing status come from the FDA National Drug Code directory -
-- the register of what is actually on the market - and classification from the
-- NLM's RxNav, which carries the WHO ATC tree, falling back to the FDA's own
-- Established Pharmacologic Class where a drug has no ATC code. Neither is
-- guessed at.
--
-- Indications, side effects and contraindications are written per *class*, not
-- per drug. An ATC or EPC class is a statement about pharmacology - every
-- proton pump inhibitor suppresses gastric acid, every loop diuretic acts on
-- the ascending limb - so one carefully written entry is right for every drug
-- underneath it. A thousand drug-by-drug entries written from memory would be a
-- thousand chances to be wrong, in the one part of this product where being
-- wrong actually hurts someone.
--
-- The trade that buys: the content is correct but general. It will tell you an
-- ACE inhibitor is for hypertension and heart failure; it will not tell you
-- what distinguishes ramipril from perindopril. Drug-specific detail stays a
-- job for a pharmacist, which is why every row here is flagged needs_review.
--
-- Doses are deliberately absent. A wrong dose is the most harmful thing this
-- product could contain and no registry field carries a single adult dose that
-- can be read out mechanically.
--
-- Existing rows are left alone: the gameplay drugs carry verified doses and
-- hand-checked clinical data that this pass must not overwrite.
--
-- 718 rather than 1000. After excluding combination products, allergenic
-- extracts, contrast media and diagnostic agents - all listed as drugs in the
-- registry, none of them medicines a pharmacy supplies to treat anything - the
-- pool of distinct single-ingredient marketed medicines with a usable
-- pharmacologic class runs to about 830. With the 163 already present that is
-- 881 in total. Reaching a round thousand would have meant counting skin-test
-- extracts as medicines.

